<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">City of St. Gallen</h1><p class="st-meta">1415 CE – 1798 CE (approx.) · capital St. Gallen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A linen-weaving city that freed itself from its famous abbey and became an imperial city and associate of the Swiss Confederacy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a> grew around the abbey founded in 719 on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall's</a> 7th-century hermitage. The townspeople gradually freed themselves from the abbot's lordship, and in 1415 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">King Sigismund</a> granted the city imperial privileges, making it a free imperial city. In 1454 it allied with six Swiss cantons as an associate (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/associates-of-the-swiss-confederacy">zugewandter Ort</a>).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a>-dominated councils ran the city, whose wealth came from linen exported across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under strict quality controls. Under its mayor and reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-vadian">Joachim Vadian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a> adopted the Reformed faith in the 1520s, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-saint-gall">Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall</a>, whose precinct lay within the town, remained Catholic. From 1566 a wall separated city and abbey.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city lost its independence in 1798 under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. In 1803 it became the capital of the new canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>, which also absorbed the abbey's lands. The abbey precinct is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/city-of-st-gallen.jpg" alt="City of St. Gallen" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: albinfo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St.Gallen_vonDreiweieren_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1415 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Gallen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joachim Vadian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free Imperial City of St. Gallen</dd></dl></aside></div>
